Q: Can we use ThreadLocal body after remove()? When can we re-read it?

One line answer:
Re-read while the request is processing; after remove(), ThreadLocal body is gone (and that request is already finished).

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Correct understanding (order matters)

No. After clear (remove()), you cannot use the ThreadLocal body anymore.

Order:
1. set(body)                    ← body available
2. filterChain.doFilter(...)    ← controller + business run HERE
   (getRawBodyOrNull() / get() works)
3. finally → remove()           ← cleared AFTER request finishes

So:
• During the request (inside doFilter) → yes, can re-read
  ◦ from wrapper (cachedBody)
  ◦ from ThreadLocal (getRawBodyOrNull() / get())
• After remove() → ThreadLocal is empty
  ◦ remove() runs only when the whole request is done, to clean up that thread

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ True / False

| Statement                                                         | True? |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- |
| Body available after set(), before remove()                       | Yes   |
| Controller / services inside doFilter can call get()              | Yes   |
| After finally remove(), ThreadLocal still has body                | No    |
| remove() runs in the middle of controller execution               | No — only after doFilter returns |
| Wrapper cachedBody also disappears after ThreadLocal remove()     | No — different storage; wrapper lives for that request object |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Timeline (draw this)

Filter starts
    │
    ├─ set(json)                 ★ ThreadLocal HAS body
    │
    ├─ doFilter(wrapper, resp)   ★ still HAS body
    │     ├─ Controller runs
    │     ├─ Service / validation may call get()
    │     └─ returns
    │
    └─ finally remove()          ★ ThreadLocal EMPTY (cleanup)

Next request on same pooled thread starts clean (no leaked old JSON).

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Example code (same filter pattern)

try {
    RequestBodyHolder.set(wrapper.getCachedBodyAsString()); // 1) available

    filterChain.doFilter(wrapper, response);                // 2) use HERE
    // inside this call:
    //   RequestBodyHolder.get()           → works
    //   wrapper.getInputStream() again    → works (cachedBody)

} finally {
    RequestBodyHolder.clear(); // 3) remove() — AFTER request finished
}

// AFTER finally:
// RequestBodyHolder.get() → null  (cannot use anymore)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Why remove() in finally?

Tomcat/Jetty reuse threads from a pool.
If you skip remove(), Thread A may still hold Request-1 JSON
when it later handles Request-2 → wrong body / memory leak.

Rule: set at start of request, remove in finally when request ends.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Wrapper vs ThreadLocal after remove()

| Storage        | During doFilter | After remove()                          |
| -------------- | --------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| ThreadLocal    | readable        | gone                                    |
| Wrapper cache  | readable        | still on wrapper object, but request is done — you normally don’t use it anymore |

Interview tip:
Don’t say “remove kills re-read forever during the request.”
Say: “remove runs after the request; during doFilter both wrapper and ThreadLocal work.”

